\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\fs28\li200\fc0\cf0 Class Cluster Description\

\fs14 \

\f0\b0\fs28\li1500 NSString objects represent character strings in OpenStep frameworks. Representing strings as objects allows you to use strings wherever you use other objects. It also provides the benefits of encapsulation, so that string objects can use whatever encoding and storage is needed for efficiency while simply appearing as arrays of characters. The cluster's two public classes, NSString and NSMutableString, declare the programmatic interface for noneditable and editable strings, respectively.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 The objects you create using these classes are referred to as 
\i string objects
\i0  (and when no confusion will result, merely as 
\i strings
\i0 ). The term 
\i C string
\i0  refers to the standard 
\b char€*
\b0  type. Because of the nature of class clusters, string objects aren't actual instances of the NSString or NSMutableString classes but of one of their private subclasses. Although a string object's class is private, its interface is public, as declared by these abstract superclasses, NSString and NSMutableString. (See ªClass Clustersº in the introduction to the Foundation Kit for more information on class clusters and on creating subclasses within a cluster.) The string classes adopt the NSCopying and NSMutableCopying protocols, making it convenient to convert a string of one type to the other.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 A string object presents itself as an array of Unicode
\f1\fs24 â
\f0\fs28  characters (Unicode is a registered trademark of Unicode, Inc.). You can determine how many characters it contains with the 
\b length
\b0  method and can retrieve a specific character with the 
\b characterAtIndex:
\b0  method. These two ªprimitiveº methods provide basic access to a string object. Most use of strings, however, is at a higher level, with the strings being treated as single entities: You compare strings against one another, search them for substrings, combine them into new strings, and so on. If you need to access string objects character-by-character, you must understand the Unicode character encoding, specifically issues related to composed character sequences. For details see:\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx2320\tx3740\tx4140\i\fs28\li2320\fc0\cf0 The Unicode Standard:  Worldwide Character Encoding
\i0 , Version 1.0, Volumes 1 and 2. The Unicode Consortium. Addison-Wesley. ISBN 0
