\fs26 \

\pard\f3\b\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 Creating and Converting String Objects\

\fs8 \

\fs6 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\f0\b0\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 NSString provides several means for creating instances, most based around the various character encodings it supports. Although string objects always present their own contents as Unicode characters, they can convert their contents to and from many other encodings, such as 7-bit ASCII, ISO Latin 1, EUC, and Shift-JIS. The 
\b availableStringEncodings
\b0  class method returns the encodings supported. You can specify an encoding explicitly when converting a C string to or from a string object, or use the 
\i default C string encoding
\i0 , which varies from platform to platform and is returned by the 
\b defaultCStringEncoding
\b0  class method.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 The simplest way to create a string object in source code is to use either the 
\b stringWithCString:
\b0  class method or the 
\b initWithCString:
\b0  instance method. Each takes a standard null-terminated C string in the default C string encoding and produces a string object. As a convenience, the Objective-C language also supports the 
\b @"..."
\b0  construct to create a string object constant from 7-bit ASCII encoding:\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx2400\tx2880\tx3420\tx3940\tx4440\tx4920\tx7000\tx7500\tx8000\tx8500\tx9020\tx9520\tx10020\tx10520\tx11020\tx11500\f4\fs24\li1900\fc0\cf0 NSString *temp = @"/tmp/scratch";\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\f0\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 Such an object is created at compile time and exists throughout your program's execution. The compiler makes such object constants unique on a per-module basis, and they're never deallocated (though you can retain and release them as you do any other object).\

\fs14 \

\fs28 To get a C string from a string object, use the 
\b cString
\b0  message. This returns a 
\b char€*
\b0  in the system's default string encoding, or raises an exception if it can't convert its contents to that encoding. The C string you receive is owned by a temporary object, though, so it will become invalid when automatic deallocation takes place (see ªObject Ownership and Automatic Disposalº in the introduction to the Foundation Kit for further information). If you want to get a permanent C string, you must create a buffer and use one of the 
\b getCString:...
\b0  methods to fill it. You can find out how large the buffer needs to be with the 
\b cStringLength
\b0  method.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 Similar methods allow you to create string objects from characters in the Unicode encoding or an arbitrary encoding, and to extract data in these encodings. 
\b initWithData:encoding:
\b0  and 
\b dataUsingEncoding:
\b0  perform these conversions from and to NSData objects. You can also read a string directly from a file in the Unicode or default C string encoding using the 
\b stringWithContentsOfFile:
\b0  class method, and write a string using 
\b writeToFile:atomically:
\b0 .\

\fs14 \

\fs28 Finally, two types of method allow you to build a string from existing string objects. 
\b localizedStringWithFormat:
\b0  and its siblings use a format string as a template into which the values you provide (string and other objects, numerics values, and so on) are inserted. The methods 
\b stringByAppendingString:
\b0  and 
\b stringByAppendingFormat:
\b0  create a new string by adding one string after another, in the second case using a format string.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 In format strings, a `%' character announces a placeholder for a value, with the characters that follow determining the kind of value expected and how to format it. For example, a format string of ª%d housesº expects an integer value to be substituted for the format expression ª%dº. NSString supports the format characters defined for the ANSI C function 
\b printf()
\b0 , plus `@' for any object (the text representation is retrieved by sending the object a 
\b description
\b0  message).\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx2300\f3\b\fs24\fi-800\li2300\fc0\cf0 Note:	
\f0\b0\fs28 Many compilers perform typecasting of arguments to 
\b printf()
\b0 , so that printing an integer into a 
\b %f
\b0  (floating-point) placeholder works as expected. This typecasting doesn't occur with NSString's formatted methods, so be sure to cast your values explicitly.\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 Value formatting is affected by the user's current locale, which is an NSDictionary specifying number, date, and other kinds of formats. NSString uses only the locale's definition for the decimal separator (given by the key named NSDecimalSeparator). If you use a method that doesn't specify a locale, the string assumes the default locale. See ªLocalesº in the ªOther Featuresº section of the Foundation Kit documentation for more information on locales.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 This table summarizes the most common means of creating and converting string objects:\

\fs14 \

