\fs26 \

\pard\f3\b\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 Working with String Objects\

\fs8 \

\fs6 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\f0\b0\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 The string classes provide methods for finding characters and substrings within strings and for comparing one string to another. These methods conform to the Unicode standard for determining whether two character sequences are equivalent. The string classes provide comparison methods that handle composed character sequences properly, though you do have the option of specifying a literal search when efficiency is important and you can guarantee some canonical form for composed character sequences.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 The search and comparison methods each come in three variants. The simplest version of each searches or compares entire strings. Other variants allow you to alter the way comparison of composed character sequences is performed and to specify a specific range of characters within a string to be searched or compared. You can specify these options (not all options are available for every method):\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx5740\tx9540\f3\b\fs28\fi-3840\li5740\fc0\cf0 Search Option	Effect\

\pard\tx6740\tx10780\fs6\li1900\fc0\cf0 \

\pard\tx5740\tx9540\f0\b0\fs28\fi-3840\li5740\fc0\cf0 NSCaseInsensitiveSearch	Ignores case distinctions among characters.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 NSLiteralSearch	Performs a byte-for-byte comparison. Differing literal sequences (such as composed character sequences) that would otherwise be considered equivalent are considered not to match. Using this option can speed some operations dramatically.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 NSBackwardsSearch	Performs searching from the end of the range toward the beginning.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 NSAnchoredSearch	Performs searching only on characters at the beginning or end of the range. No match at the beginning or end means nothing is found, even if a matching sequence of characters occurs elsewhere in the string.\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx2300\f3\b\fs24\fi-800\li2300\fc0\cf0 Note:	
\f0\b0\fs28 Search and comparison are currently performed as if the NSLiteralSearch option were specified. As the Unicode encoding becomes more widely used, and the need for more flexible comparison increases, the default behavior will be changed accordingly.\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 Substrings are only found if completely contained within the specified range. If you specify a range for a search or comparison method and don't request NSLiteralSearch, the range must not break composed character sequences on either end; if it does you could get an incorrect result. (See the method description for 
\b rangeOfComposedCharacterSequenceAtIndex:
\b0  for a code sample that adjusts a range to lie on character sequence boundaries.)\

\fs14 \

\fs28 The basic search and comparison methods are these:\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx7500\fs28\li1900\fc0\cf0 rangeOfString:	compare:\
rangeOfString:options:	compare:options:\
rangeOfStrings:options:range:	compare:options:range:\

\fs14 \

\fs28 rangeOfCharacterFromSet:\
rangeOfCharacterFromSet:options:\
rangeOfCharacterFromSet:options:range:\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 The 
\b rangeOfString:...
\b0  methods search for a substring within the receiver. The 
\b rangeOfCharacterFromSet:...
\b0  methods search for individual characters from a supplied set of characters. The 
\b compare:...
\b0  methods return the lexical ordering of the receiver and the supplied string. Several other methods allow you to determine whether two strings are equal or whether one is the prefix or suffix of another, but they don't have variants that allow you to specify search options or ranges.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 In addition to searching and comparing strings, you can combine and divide them in various ways. The simplest way to put two strings together is to append one to the other. The 
\b stringByAppendingString:
\b0  method returns a string object formed from the receiver and the argument supplied. You can also combine several strings according to a template with the 
\b initWithFormat:
\b0 , 
\b stringWithFormat:
\b0  and 
\b stringByAppendingFormat:
\b0  methods. See ªCreating and Converting String Objectsº for more information.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 You can extract substrings from the beginning or end of a string to a particular index, or from a specific range, with the 
\b substringToIndex:
\b0 , 
\b substringFromIndex:
\b0 , and 
\b substringWithRange:
\b0  methods. You can also split a string into substrings (based on a separator string) with the 
\b componentsSeparatedByString:
\b0  method.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 Most of the NSString classes' remaining methods are for conveniences such as changing case, quickly extracting numeric values, and working with encodings. There's also a set of methods for treating strings as file system paths, described below in ªManipulating Paths.º An additional class cluster, NSScanner, allows you to scan a string object for numeric and string values. Both the NSString and the NSScanner class clusters use the NSCharacterSet class cluster for search operations. See the appropriate class specifications for more information.\

\fs14 \

\fs26 \

\pard\f3\b\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 Manipulating Paths\

\fs8 \

\fs6 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\f0\b0\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 In addition to all the basic methods for working with character strings merely as strings, NSString also provides a rich set of methods for manipulating strings as file system paths. A string can extract a path's directory, file name, and extension, expand a tilde expression (such as ª~meº) or create one for the user's home directory, and clean up paths containing symbolic links, redundant slashes, and references to ª.º (current directory) and ª..º (parent directory). These methods are listed under ªWorking with pathsº in the ªMethodTypesº section.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 NSString represents paths generically with `/' as the path separator and `.' as the extension separator. Methods that accept strings as path arguments convert these generic representations to the proper system-specific form as needed. On systems with an implicit root directory, absolute paths begin with a path separator or with a tilde expression (ª~/...º or ª~
\i user
\i0 /...º). On systems that require explicit expression of root directories for different devices, such as Microsoft Windows 95, absolute paths begin with the name of the device (for example, ªC:/Documents/Paper.docº to represent the actual path ªC:\\Documents\\Paper.docº). Where a device must be specified, you can do that yourselfÐintroducing a system dependencyÐor allow the string object to add a default device.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 \

\fs14 \

